      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Specific immunotherapy (SIT) safety has been well documented. However, the prolonged late side effects in patients who terminated SIT several years previously have been reported on in only a limited number of studies. The aim of this study was to perform a 20-year post-SIT observational evaluation for the assessment of any manifestation of serious immunological disease."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "In total, 1,144 patients (521 women and 623 men), with a mean age of 22.8 ± 16.9 years (at the moment of SIT completion) and who had atopic bronchial asthma and/or allergic rhinitis, were observed 20 years after immunotherapy. New neoplastic and autoimmune disease cases were monitored. The SIT group was compared to a control group consisting of 1,154 allergic patients who had never received SIT and had only had symptomatic treatment."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There was an inverse association between SIT treatment and the prevalence of new chronic myeloid leukemia and chronic lymphoblastic leukemia cases (OR 0.32, 95% CI 0.18-0.81 and OR 0.58, CI 0.44-0.78, respectively). In other neoplastic diseases, however, prevalences similar to those observed in the control group were confirmed. There were also no significant differences in the autoimmune disease prevalence between the analyzed groups."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The results of this long-term observational study indicate a lack of a significant prevalence for new instances of neoplastic and autoimmune diseases, which suggests that SIT in the long term is indeed safe."
          }
        ]
      },
